---
/**
 * website/src/components/HomeSlider.astro — the homepage's slider (the owner's approved draft
 * "Home, draft 2 (slider)", 2026-10-19; the owner's feedback of 2026-10-21, item 3, which
 * turned the strip of tabs into a slider).
 *
 * **One block, three slides, three dots, two step controls and a clock.** The draft put the
 * whole of what the page has to show into one block with three tabs, and the owner's reading of
 * the live site asked for that strip to behave like a slider: it advances by itself every few
 * seconds, stops while a reader is in it or has asked their system for less motion, steps back
 * and forward, and shows both where the reader is and how many slides there are. The three
 * panels are the same three, in the same order, with the same headings.
 *
 * **The script is an enhancement and nothing is built at runtime.** Every panel is in the HTML
 * a reader receives, the dots are fragment links to the panels' own ids, and the stylesheet's
 * `:target` rules are what shows the one a reader chose — so a reader whose browser runs no
 * script (or whose script never arrives) still has all three slides and a way to open each of
 * them. What the script adds is the clock, the two step controls and the mark on the current
 * dot; the two controls are emitted `hidden` and revealed by it, the way the copy control is
 * (D183's "no broken control": a reader without the script is shown no control that does
 * nothing). `website/test/slider.mjs` reads both states — the no-script one out of the markup
 * and the stylesheet, and the script's own out of the file the site ships and out of a run of
 * it against the built page.
 *
 * **Dots rather than radio inputs, and the site's own privacy page is the reason.** A radio
 * group is the other way to write this pattern and it reads well to a screen reader: the group
 * is announced as three choices and the arrow keys move between them. What it costs is a form
 * control on a page — three of them — and this site states, in `privacy.astro` and in the gate
 * that holds the page to the artifact (`test/consent.mjs`), that it has **no form and asks a
 * reader for nothing**. That claim is about collection, and a radio that switches a panel
 * collects nothing — but it is a form control, and the sentence and the gate are the owner's,
 * not this component's to spend. So every control here is a link or a button:
 * `href="#home-panel-…"` for the dots, which every browser has understood since HTML 2, no
 * form, no name, nothing submitted.
 *
 * **No ARIA tab roles, on purpose.** `role="tab"` and `role="tabpanel"` describe a widget whose
 * selection is scripted: the roles promise a relationship (`aria-selected`, `aria-controls`,
 * the panel that follows the selected tab) that only JavaScript can keep, and the panels here
 * are reachable with the script off. The markup says what it is instead — three links, each
 * pointing at the panel it opens, each named for its slide and its place in the three — and the
 * one state the script does write is `aria-current="true"` on the dot whose slide is showing,
 * which is a plain statement about a link rather than a promise about a widget.
 *
 * **The three panels.** *Easy to use* is the reader's three steps, numbered, one inline SVG
 * each: tell your agent, Fugaro runs it in your cloud, you get a draft pull request — the
 * draft's own three lines, each with the repository's sentence about it under it, held to
 * README.md at build time the way the flow's captions are held to theirs (Global Constraint
 * 6). *Why Fugaro?* is `Benefits.astro` unchanged: the README's four reasons, with their icons,
 * in the order the README argues them. *See it* is the console and then the real `fugaro watch`
 * capture — the owner's own screenshot of the screen running on their installation — with the
 * one command the reader is asked for under it.
 *
 * **The "See it" panel is a slider of its own, and it is `ScreenshotSlider.astro`** (the brief
 * of 2026-10-21: "replace the single Fugaro Watch screenshot with a slider"; D246, D251, D252).
 * The panel used to hold one `<img>` — the owner's `fugaro watch` capture — and it now holds the
 * console page's component, rendering `website/data/home-screens.yaml`: the console's own three
 * screenshots first, because the brief asks the desktop console to lead and take the most space,
 * and the watch capture last as the "for the terminal crowd" slide. That component was the
 * console page's alone until this round; the three facts about the page it is rendered on — its
 * heading, its `data-slot` and its slide-id prefix — are props now, and the homepage passes its
 * own. **What the panel keeps is the slot**: `div[data-slot="watch-screenshot"]` is still the
 * element after the panel's heading, it is still the one the design record points at, and the
 * slider renders inside it.
 *
 * **Two sliders, one script, and they are told apart by what they hold.** The block above is a
 * deck of three panels; the slider inside the third panel is a scroll container of four slides.
 * Both are `[data-slider]`, both carry the same interior hooks (`[data-slider-track]`,
 * `[data-slider-slide]`, `[data-slider-dot]`, the two step controls, the count), and both are
 * driven by the one file — the console's driver moves the track and reads its position back, and
 * the homepage's driver moves panels by name. `site.js`'s `own()` is what keeps each driver on
 * its own block's elements, and `website/test/slider.mjs` reads the block's three panels and
 * three dots with the same rule, so the case and the script cannot count differently.
 *
 * **Why the panel is the block and the section inside it is not.** The benefits panel renders
 * `Benefits.astro`'s section, which carries its own gutter and its own room below (that is the
 * round-6 fix, and it is right when a page renders the section as a block of its own). Inside a
 * panel the panel is the block: the two gutters would stack, and the section is 32 px narrower
 * than the other two panels' text. So the section is rendered with `inPanel`, which is the
 * section's own rule standing its gutter down — declared in `Benefits.astro`, where the element
 * is rendered, rather than reached into from here with a `:global` selector.
 */
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import path from 'node:path'
import { REPO_ROOT, loadHomeScreens } from '../content.ts'
import { copyControlHTML } from '../lib/copy-control.mjs'
import Benefits from './Benefits.astro'
import ScreenshotSlider from './ScreenshotSlider.astro'

/**
 * The slides the "See it" panel shows, read from `website/data/home-screens.yaml` through the
 * content pipeline: the console's own three screenshots and then the `fugaro watch` capture,
 * with each row's `alt`, caption and own pixel size (D246, D251, D252). Read here rather than
 * handed in as a prop, because the panel is the only caller and the file is the panel's — the
 * way this component already reads the README.md sections its steps quote.
 */
const HOME_SCREENS = loadHomeScreens().screens

/**
 * A string with its markdown markers and its whitespace removed: how a caption and the section
 * it claims to come from are compared. The same reading `ArchitectureDiagram.astro` uses, so
 * the build and `website/test/home.mjs` resolve a quotation the same way.
 */
function comparable(document: string): string {
  return document.replace(/[`*]/g, '').replace(/\s+/g, '')
}

/**
 * One `## <heading>` section of a document of this repository, up to the next `## `. A section
 * rather than the whole file: two of the three steps quote README.md, and held to the file
 * alone they could be shuffled among the steps with each still a quotation. A heading the
 * document does not carry is a defect in this file rather than in the document, so it fails
 * here, naming itself.
 */
function section(document: string, heading: string): string {
  const text = readFileSync(path.join(REPO_ROOT, document), 'utf8')
  const at = [...text.matchAll(/^## (.+)$/gm)].find((match) => match[1].replace(/`/g, '') === heading)
  if (!at || at.index === undefined) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/components/HomeSlider.astro: ${document} carries no "## ${heading}" section, and a step's line is quoted from it (the owner's approved draft of 2026-10-19; Global Constraint 6). Either the section was renamed or the step's line moved.`,
    )
  }
  const rest = text.slice(at.index)
  const next = rest.indexOf('\n## ', 1)
  return next === -1 ? rest : rest.slice(0, next)
}

/** The sections a step's line may be quoted from, by the name the step cites. */
const SOURCES: { [name: string]: string } = {
  'README.md#Getting started': section('README.md', 'Getting started'),
  'README.md#Why Fugaro': section('README.md', 'Why Fugaro'),
}

/**
 * The three steps, in the draft's order, each with the section its line is a line of. The
 * names are the draft's own three ("you tell your agent, Fugaro runs it in your cloud, you get
 * a draft pull request"); the lines are the repository's sentences about them, shortened to
 * what fits under a name.
 */
const STEPS = [
  {
    id: 'agent',
    name: 'Tell your agent',
    source: 'README.md#Getting started',
    // README.md's own sentence about where a task goes in: the reader's agent, through the
    // plugin's skill, rather than a command they type.
    line: "the plugin's working skill launches, watches and diagnoses runs",
    icon: 'bubble',
  },
  {
    id: 'cloud',
    name: 'Fugaro runs it in your cloud',
    source: 'README.md#Why Fugaro',
    // The first of the README's four reasons, and the sentence in it about where the work
    // happens: the reader's own project, in a container that is thrown away.
    line: 'It runs in a throwaway container in your own cloud project',
    icon: 'cloud',
  },
  {
    id: 'pull-request',
    name: 'You get a draft pull request',
    source: 'README.md#Why Fugaro',
    // The same reason's sentence about what comes back, draft state included: the reader does
    // not get a log to read, they get a pull request to review.
    line: 'Every run that pushed ends in a pull request, a draft one if it failed',
    icon: 'pull-request',
  },
]

/**
 * The three steps' drawings, by name: inline SVG, `currentColor`, the accent from the rule
 * below. A map rather than a branch in the template, for the reason `Benefits.astro`'s glyphs
 * are a map: a name whose entry holds no shape renders an empty `<svg>` that nothing can see,
 * and the guard at the foot of the frontmatter refuses one.
 */
const GLYPHS: { [name: string]: string } = {
  // A message: what the reader says to their agent.
  bubble: '<path d="M4 5h16v11H9l-5 4V5Z"/>',
  // A cloud: where the run happens.
  cloud: '<path d="M7 18h9a4 4 0 0 0 .6-7.95A5.5 5.5 0 0 0 6 11.2 3.5 3.5 0 0 0 7 18Z"/>',
  // A pull request: two commits and the branch between them.
  'pull-request':
    '<circle cx="6" cy="6" r="2.5"/><circle cx="6" cy="18" r="2.5"/><circle cx="18" cy="18" r="2.5"/><path d="M6 8.5v7"/><path d="M18 15.5V11a3 3 0 0 0-3-3h-4"/><path d="m13 6-2 2 2 2"/>',
}

/**
 * The slides, in the order a reader meets them: the draft's three, and the label each one
 * carries. Each one's `id` is the panel it shows (`#home-panel-<id>`) and the fragment its dot
 * links to, and the name the script writes on the root when it is the slide on screen — so the
 * dot, the link, the panel and the script's state are one name rather than four that have to
 * agree. The label is the panel's own heading and the name its dot is announced with.
 */
const SLIDES = [
  { id: 'easy', label: 'Easy to use' },
  { id: 'benefits', label: 'Why Fugaro?' },
  { id: 'see', label: 'See it' },
]

/**
 * The one command the reader is asked for, and the skill's own line about what the screen
 * shows. Both are the site's existing pair — the README prints the command, the `working` skill
 * writes the note — and both are held to those files below rather than typed here.
 */
const WATCH = 'fugaro watch'
const WATCH_TAIL = 'is the live screen of spend, repositories and running agents.'
const WATCH_NOTE = `\`${WATCH}\` ${WATCH_TAIL}`

// Hold every step's line to the section it cites, in that section's order, here, so a
// paraphrase — or a line that moved to the step above — stops the build naming the file rather
// than reaching a reader as the repository's own words. Two of the three cite README.md's
// `## Why Fugaro`, and `cursor` walks forward through the section, so they can only be quoted
// in the order the section writes them.
{
  const cursors: { [name: string]: number } = {}
  for (const [index, step] of STEPS.entries()) {
    const document = comparable(SOURCES[step.source] ?? '')
    const line = comparable(step.line)
    const from = cursors[step.source] ?? 0
    const at = document.indexOf(line, from)
    if (at === -1) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/components/HomeSlider.astro: step ${index + 1}'s line ${JSON.stringify(step.line)} is not a line of ${step.source} at or after the step before it, and every line under a step is the repository's own words in the order its document writes them (the owner's approved draft of 2026-10-19; Global Constraint 6).`,
      )
    }
    cursors[step.source] = at + line.length
  }
}

// Every step carries a glyph, and a glyph holds a shape: an entry with nothing in it draws an
// empty `<svg>`, which is a step with no icon at all.
for (const step of STEPS) {
  if (!GLYPHS[step.icon]) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/components/HomeSlider.astro: step ${JSON.stringify(step.name)} is drawn with ${JSON.stringify(step.icon)}, which is no glyph this component draws (${Object.keys(GLYPHS).join(', ')}): the draft asks each step for an icon, and a glyph the step names and the component has no drawing for is a step rendered with an empty <svg>.`,
    )
  }
}

// The command and the note, held to the files that write them: `fugaro watch` is a command the
// documentation prints (Global Constraint 9), and the sentence under it is the skill's rather
// than one written for the page (Global Constraint 6).
if (!readFileSync(path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'README.md'), 'utf8').includes(WATCH)) {
  throw new Error(
    `website/src/components/HomeSlider.astro: README.md does not print ${JSON.stringify(WATCH)}, and the block offers a command the documentation carries. Either the README stopped documenting it or the block is showing one that does not exist (Global Constraint 9).`,
  )
}
if (
  !comparable(readFileSync(path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'plugin/skills/working/SKILL.md'), 'utf8')).includes(
    comparable(WATCH_NOTE),
  )
) {
  throw new Error(
    `website/src/components/HomeSlider.astro: the note ${JSON.stringify(WATCH_NOTE)} is not a line of plugin/skills/working/SKILL.md, and the page quotes the skill rather than describing what the screen does (Global Constraint 6).`,
  )
}
---

<section class="home-slider" data-slot="home-slider" data-slider aria-label="What Fugaro is and does">
  {/* The block's own heading, and the line the board of 2026-10-20 draws over the control:
      what a reader gets out of the three panels under it. It is the site's own sentence
      rather than a document's — it names what the block shows, and no document of this
      repository says it — so it is listed with the page's other new lines in the pull
      request for the owner to veto. */}
  <h2 class="slider-title">Say it. Get pull requests.</h2>

  {/* The controls: one step back, one dot per slide, one step on. The dots are the three links
      the tabs used to be — each one a fragment link to the panel it shows, which is the whole
      of the switching with no script — and they are the control that says where the reader is,
      so they carry the slide's own name and its place in the three. The two buttons are the
      script's: they are emitted `hidden` and revealed by it, because a step control that steps
      nowhere is the broken control D183 keeps off the site. The whole row is a list of
      controls and not a list of prose, and it wraps rather than scrolling on a phone. */}
  <div class="slider-controls">
    <button type="button" class="slider-step" data-slider-prev aria-label="Previous slide" hidden>
      <svg
        class="slider-arrow"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="2"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
        aria-hidden="true"
        focusable="false"
      >
        <path d="m15 5-7 7 7 7" />
      </svg>
    </button>

    <ul class="slider-dots">
      {
        SLIDES.map((slide, index) => (
          <li class="slider-dot-item">
            {/* The dot's own mark is the anchor's pseudo-element, so the anchor is the target a
                reader taps and the mark is the small circle drawn inside it. The name is the
                slide and the reader's place in the three, which is what a dot shows and what a
                screen reader cannot see. */}
            <a
              class="slider-dot"
              href={`#home-panel-${slide.id}`}
              data-slider-dot={slide.id}
              aria-label={`Slide ${index + 1} of ${SLIDES.length}: ${slide.label}`}
            />
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <button type="button" class="slider-step" data-slider-next aria-label="Next slide" hidden>
      <svg
        class="slider-arrow"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="2"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
        aria-hidden="true"
        focusable="false"
      >
        <path d="m9 5 7 7-7 7" />
      </svg>
    </button>
  </div>

  <div class="panels">
    <div class="panel panel-easy" id="home-panel-easy" data-slider-slide="easy">
      <h2 class="panel-title">Easy to use</h2>
      <ol class="easy-steps">
        {
          STEPS.map((step, index) => (
            <li class="easy-step" data-step={step.id} data-source={step.source}>
              {/* The step's place in the list, as a number: the `<ol>` carries the order, so
                  this is decoration and a screen reader reads the list instead. */}
              <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">
                {index + 1}
              </span>
              <svg
                class="step-icon"
                viewBox="0 0 24 24"
                fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                aria-hidden="true"
                focusable="false"
              >
                <Fragment set:html={GLYPHS[step.icon]} />
              </svg>
              <h3 class="step-name">{step.name}</h3>
              <p class="step-line">{step.line}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="panel panel-benefits" id="home-panel-benefits" data-slider-slide="benefits">
      {/* `Benefits.astro` renders its own heading and its own section; inside a panel the panel
          is the block, so the section stands its gutter down (see the header). */}
      <Benefits inPanel />
    </div>

    <div class="panel panel-see" id="home-panel-see" data-slider-slide="see">
      <h2 class="panel-title">See it</h2>

      {/* The slot the owner's capture goes into. `data-slot` is the hook
          `website/test/home.mjs` reads; it is deliberately not a class, so it is not something
          the stylesheet draws. The image is served from this site (no third-party host), its
          `alt` is the gist of the transcription that ships beside it
          (`public/images/fugaro-watch.txt`), and the declared dimensions are the capture's
          logical size — 2918 x 882 px taken at 2x — so the browser reserves the right box and
          nothing under the picture moves when the file lands. `loading="lazy"`: the panel is
          the third slide and the picture is not on the first screen. */}
      <div class="watch-shot" data-slot="watch-screenshot">
        <ScreenshotSlider
          screens={HOME_SCREENS}
          heading="Screenshots"
          slot="home-screens"
          idPrefix="home-screen"
          label="Screenshots of Fugaro. Scroll sideways to see the rest."
        />
      </div>

      <div class="watch-block command-block" data-copy>
        <Fragment set:html={copyControlHTML('Copy the command')} />
        <pre class="watch-command"><code>{WATCH}</code></pre>
      </div>

      {/* The skill's own line about the screen, with the command in the site's code style
          rather than in markdown backticks (the two parts are held to the skill above). */}
      <p class="watch-note">
        <code>{WATCH}</code> {WATCH_TAIL}
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): this component names the palette, the spacing scale, the type scale and
     the radii, and defines none of them.

     **The block's own gutter and its room below are declared here**, like the page's other
     top-level blocks: the hero pads below itself and the install section after this one opens
     with no top padding, so the gap between two blocks is the block above's own bottom padding
     and nothing pads one gap twice (`website/test/base-styles.mjs`'s `homeRhythmFailures`).
     The panels are inside this block and carry no gutter of their own. */
  .home-slider {
    position: relative;
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-md) var(--space-3xl);
  }

  /* The block's heading, at the display scale's middle step: the board draws the line over the
     control at the same weight and tracking as the page's other section headings, one step
     under the hero's own. It carries the room below it that separates it from the control. */
  .slider-title {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    color: var(--text);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-2xl), 4.4vw, var(--font-size-4xl));
    font-weight: var(--weight-black);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* The row the controls stand in: one step back, the three dots, one step on, with the row
     wrapping rather than scrolling on a phone. `website/test/slider.mjs` reads the wrap and the
     44 px target every control in it keeps. */
  .slider-controls {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }

  /* The two step controls. They are drawn by the script and `hidden` until it runs, so this
     rule declares the box the arrow is centred in and the rule below is what keeps the markup's
     `hidden` in force against it — an author `display` wins over the user agent's `[hidden]`
     rule, and without that pair a reader whose browser runs no script would be shown two
     buttons that step nowhere. It is the pair `ConsentBanner.astro` keeps its own banner with,
     and the reason `base.css`'s `.copy-button` declares no `display` at all. */
  .slider-step {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-width: 44px;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0;
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--panel);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .slider-step[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  .slider-arrow {
    width: var(--space-sm);
    height: var(--space-sm);
  }

  /* The dots: a list with its markers off, because it is a row of three controls and not a
     list of prose. */
  .slider-dots {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .slider-dot-item {
    display: flex;
  }

  /* A dot is a target a reader taps, so it is at least 44 px of box with the mark drawn inside
     it: the board draws a 12 px circle, and a 12 px target is not one a finger can hit (the
     owner's brief of 2026-10-19 measures every control against 44 px). It is written as a
     minimum rather than a size, because that is the declaration the site's one reading of the
     brief looks for — `website/test/lib/controls.mjs` — and because the box may grow. The same
     shape, and the same rule, as the console page's screenshot slider. */
  .slider-dot {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-width: 44px;
    min-height: 44px;
  }

  /* The mark itself, drawn by the anchor's own pseudo-element so that the target above is
     invisible and what a reader sees is the small circle. */
  .slider-dot::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: var(--space-xs);
    height: var(--space-xs);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  /* **The dot of the slide a reader is looking at**, and the two ways it is decided. Without
     the script it is the fragment's: the panel the address names, or the first one while it
     names none — the same pair of rules that shows the panel, read on the dot instead. With the
     script it is the script's own word, `aria-current="true"` on the dot whose slide is
     showing, which is also what a screen reader reads out.

     The first four selectors stand down the moment the script writes `data-active` on the root,
     and that is not a tidy-up: a reader who arrived on `#home-panel-see` and then let the block
     advance would otherwise see two dots marked — the fragment's, which does not move, and the
     script's, which does.

     **They are in an `@supports selector(:has(*))` rule of their own, and the script's own
     selector is not.** All four of them ask for `:has()`, and a selector list is not forgiving:
     beside `.slider-dot[aria-current='true']` a browser too old to answer `:has()` would drop the
     rule and take the script's mark down with it, which is the dot a reader who lets the block
     advance follows. The same reason, and the same fix, as the first panel's rule below;
     `website/test/slider.mjs` reads the built stylesheet for both. */
  @supports selector(:has(*)) {
    .home-slider:not([data-active]):not(:has(.panel:target)) .slider-dot[href='#home-panel-easy']::before,
    .home-slider:not([data-active]):has(#home-panel-easy:target) .slider-dot[href='#home-panel-easy']::before,
    .home-slider:not([data-active]):has(#home-panel-benefits:target) .slider-dot[href='#home-panel-benefits']::before,
    .home-slider:not([data-active]):has(#home-panel-see:target) .slider-dot[href='#home-panel-see']::before {
      border-color: var(--accent);
      background: var(--accent);
    }
  }

  .slider-dot[aria-current='true']::before {
    border-color: var(--accent);
    background: var(--accent);
  }

  /* **The slides, and the whole of the switching.** Every panel is in the HTML a reader
     receives; the stylesheet shows one and takes the other two out of the layout.

     `display` is the one property these rules decide, at every width and never inside a query
     (`website/test/base-styles.mjs` refuses a `display` on a panel inside a media query, because
     the viewport is not what chooses a slide), and the block is read in both of its states:
     the script's, keyed on the root's `data-active`, and the fragment's, keyed on `:target` and
     standing down while `data-active` is there.

     The focus ring is not declared here: `base.css`'s `:focus-visible` rule draws the site's
     ring on every control, and a link and a button are both controls, so a dot or a step the
     keyboard is on carries the ring the rest of the site carries. It is the accent at the
     token's width and offset, which is visible against the page and against the control's own
     ground — and a rule here that removed it would be the one thing `a11y.mjs` refuses. */
  .panel {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The script's state: one rule per slide, so the name the script writes on the root and the
     panel it shows are one name rather than two that have to agree.

     The no-script `:target` rule is in this list rather than beside the `:has()` rule below it,
     and that placement is load-bearing: a selector list is not forgiving, so one selector a
     browser cannot read takes the whole rule down with it. Neither of the four names `:has()`, so
     a browser too old to answer `:has()` still shows the panel a dot opens — and it still shows
     the panel the script's own name points at, which a list carrying the `:has()` selector would
     have taken down with it. */
  .home-slider[data-active='easy'] #home-panel-easy,
  .home-slider[data-active='benefits'] #home-panel-benefits,
  .home-slider[data-active='see'] #home-panel-see,
  .home-slider:not([data-active]) .panel:target {
    display: block;
  }

  /* The no-script state's other half: the first panel, while the fragment names none — a reader
     who has clicked nothing is reading the first thing the block has to say. It stands down while
     the script is driving.

     **It is inside `@supports selector(:has(*))` rather than beside the rule above, and that
     nesting is load-bearing.** It is the one selector in the block that asks for `:has()`, and a
     selector list is not forgiving: one selector a browser cannot read takes the whole rule down
     with it. Written as a sibling of the rule above it is not safe either, because the build
     merges adjacent rules that carry the same declaration — `display: block` is what both rules
     say, and the built stylesheet put all five selectors back into one rule when this was tried,
     which is the defect again with a smaller diff. An at-rule cannot be merged into its sibling,
     so a browser too old to answer `:has()` drops this rule and nothing else: what such a reader
     loses is the panel shown before they touch a dot, and never the panel every dot opens, and
     never the panel the script's own name points at. `website/test/slider.mjs` reads the built
     stylesheet for exactly that: no rule may carry a `:has()` selector beside one that has none. */
  @supports selector(:has(*)) {
    .home-slider:not([data-active]):not(:has(.panel:target)) #home-panel-easy {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  /* The panel's own box: the panel tone, the hairline border and the larger radius, with the
     room the board gives a panel around its content. It is on every panel rather than on the
     visible one, so moving on changes the contents and not the box.

     It is a rule of its own, and it names `.panel` and nothing else about the block, for two
     reasons the readings in `website/test/` make concrete. A selector that named the block
     would be read by `base-styles.mjs`'s `homeRhythmFailures` as the block's own padding, and
     the slider would read as a block that opens with its own 48 px over it on top of the room
     the hero leaves below it. And a selector naming a targeted panel would be read by
     `slider.mjs` as a second rule showing a panel. */
  .panel {
    box-sizing: border-box;
    min-height: 24rem;
    padding: var(--space-xl) var(--space-lg);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-xl);
    background: var(--panel);
  }

  /* The panels' own label. Each panel carries the label of the slide it is, so a reader who
     arrives on a panel — by the dots, by the script's clock, by a screen reader or in the HTML —
     reads what they are looking at; it is set at the display scale's middle step, so the panel
     opens with a heading rather than with a line of body type. */
  .panel-title {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: clamp(var(--font-size-xl), 3vw, var(--font-size-3xl));
    font-weight: var(--weight-black);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The three steps: one column, each step a row of number, glyph, name and line. The measure
     is the site's, because a step's line is prose. */
  .easy-steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* The step is a card on the panel it is drawn in, since the bold redesign: the surface tone
     one step above the panel, the hairline border and the larger radius the boards give a card.
     Its grid, its padding and its border width are what `website/test/base-styles.mjs`'s
     `sliderLayoutFailures` reads to work out whether the longest step name fits a phone, so the
     restyle is the tones and the radius and nothing else. */
  .easy-step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--space-sm);
    row-gap: var(--space-3xs);
    align-items: center;
    padding: var(--space-sm);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  /* The number and the glyph are the row's first two cells and the name and the line are the
     third: the two marks stay in their own columns whatever the text does, which is what keeps
     the three names starting at one edge down the list. */
  .step-number {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 1 / span 2;
    color: var(--accent-on-dark);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  .step-icon {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1 / span 2;
    width: 28px;
    height: 28px;
    color: var(--accent-on-dark);
  }

  .step-name {
    grid-column: 3;
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .step-line {
    grid-column: 3;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }

  /* The slides' slot, and nothing of its own: the slider inside it is `ScreenshotSlider.astro`,
     which draws its own frame, its own track, its own dots and its own captions (the owner's
     feedback of 2026-10-21, D246). The slot takes no space while it is empty — the rule the
     asset's own task left behind, kept so a slot that lost its slides again is not a blank
     rectangle — and it is the hook `website/test/home.mjs` reads the rows of. The rule that
     drew the single `<img>` this slot used to hold is gone with the `<img>`: a picture drawn by
     a rule nothing renders is a rule that says the panel shows something it does not. */
  .watch-shot:empty {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The command bar, and the one bar around it: `base.css`'s `.command-block` lays the command
     at the left and its control at the right, and this rule is the bar's own look — the ground
     the board draws a terminal on, the hairline border and the radius the site's other command
     bars carry. */
  .watch-block {
    padding: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-sm);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: var(--ground);
  }

  .watch-command {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--accent-on-dark);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  }

  .watch-note {
    margin: var(--space-xs) 0 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
  }
</style>
